Tell both concurrent retirements the earliest instant, as ADR-0020 §5 specifies (#150) (#151)

* fix(mcp): re-query the directory for the earliest retirement instant (#150)

ADR-0020 §5 accepts that two genuinely concurrent first retirements of one
run can both find no record and both append, and states the reconciliation:
both callers are told the same instant, because the directory answers with
the earliest. retire()'s winning-path branch returned retiredAt straight
from its own s.record() append and never performed that re-query, so two
concurrent callers were told two different instants.

Confirmed with a deterministic rendezvous (armRendezvous) that holds two
goroutines at CredentialRun until both have read "not retired" before either
appends — TestMCP019ConcurrentFirstRetirementsReportTheEarliestInstant fails
against the pre-fix code with two different retired_at values and passes
after retire() re-reads the directory via the new earliestRetiredAt.

New test IDs (not added to doc 07): MCP-019.

* test(mcp): cover earliestRetiredAt's defensive error paths (#150)

The post-append re-query added for ADR-0020 §5 has three refusal paths that
the shipped rundir.Directory cannot itself trigger (it reads the same chain
this tool just wrote to), but IP §2's 100%-branch floor asks for every
error-return path of every MCP tool regardless. answerAtCall makes each one
reachable: the re-query failing, the directory forgetting the run, and the
directory reporting the run not retired, all immediately after this call's
own successful append.